  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nTemperatures are tumbling out there this morning with mid 30s\njust after midnight for much of northeast Minnesota north of US\n2. Temperatures are a little slower to fall in northwest\nWisconsin with a few clouds lingering, but expect them to fall\nwithin the next couple hours. Frost Advisories and Freeze\nWarnings will remain in effect through mid morning. \n\nHigh pressure will depart to the east today and strong warm air\nadvection and a warm front will move in this afternoon and\nevening. There is increasing confidence that an area of showers\nwill accompany the warm front from west to east late this\nafternoon into this evening. PWATs will increase to around 0.75\nto 1\" along with generally weak synoptic forcing with the warm\nfront. Lacking any other substantial upper-level forcing\nmechanisms, along with lingering low-level dry air not being shy\nto go away, whatever rain does fall should be pretty light. Some\nsubtle instability will likely make these quasi-convective\nshowers, but thunder is not expected.\n\nTuesday is looking to be an unseasonably warm with highs running\naround 15 degrees above average. Sunshine should be plentiful as\nwe sit within the dry warm sector of a low pressure system well\nto the north in Ontario. A cold front will swing through Tuesday\nlater afternoon and night. An upper level trough on Wednesday\ncould bring a few sprinkles, but otherwise dry weather is likely\nwith slightly cooler, but still mild temperatures. \n\nNorthwest flow will persist through Thursday with quiet weather,\nthen perhaps a wind change to southeasterlies Friday. Some cool\nnights Thursday night and Friday night could mean more\nfrost/freeze conditions. Another low pressure system may make \nits way through this weekend, and if the track remains as \ncurrently suggested by most models, we would end up in the warm\nsector with a pretty mild weekend and perhaps a little rain for\nsome places. Early next week, there are some early hints of a \nsurge of cold air behind this potential low, which could cause \ntemperatures to tumble closer to normal.",
